<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crypteia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Krypteia, Krupteia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crypteia was an institution of the ancient Spartan state, made up of young upper-class men, probably aged between 21 and 30, who had passed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoge">agoge</a> with distinction. Ancient accounts differ on its purpose: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and Heraclides Lembus, possibly following a lost work by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, describe kryptai sent into the countryside with daggers to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> declared war on the helots each autumn, while others saw it as military training or an initiation ordeal. Aristotle credited it to the lawgiver Lycurgus, but Plutarch thought it was introduced, if at all, after the helot revolt of the mid-460s BCE. Modern historians often translate the name as secret police.</p></div>
